+package registry
+
+// Deckhouse: the part of Store that serves the scope filter.
+//
+// Store.ScopeFilter, when non-nil, adds Deckhouse's generic
+// `-A --scope=<kind>` cross-namespace filtering to List/Get/Watch. Its own
+// Trigger decides whether a given request actually gets routed through it
+// (scopefilter.Decorator.Engaged) -- a resource with ScopeFilter wired but
+// Trigger returning false for a given ctx (e.g. no --scope header sent) sees
+// byte-for-byte vanilla behavior at zero extra cost.
+//
+// The field lives on Store itself, rather than requiring every resource's own
+// REST wrapper type to add matching fields and List/Get/Watch overrides, so
+// that covering another built-in resource (or a CustomResourceDefinition --
+// apiextensions-apiserver's customresource.REST also wraps *Store) costs a
+// construction-time assignment plus a scopefilter.Register call, not a
+// per-resource file edit repeated on every version bump.
+//
+// Get is split into Get and getIgnoringScopeFilter, the original body: once
+// ScopeFilter has decided a request is allowed, it performs the actual read
+// through getIgnoringScopeFilter, without going back through Get's own
+// ScopeFilter check, which would recurse forever.
+
+// DeckhouseScopeStore returns the underlying *Store so the central
+// scope-filter wiring loop (pkg/controlplane/apiserver) can reach it through
+// an interface assertion. A REST type that EMBEDS *Store promotes this
+// method and is thus auto-covered; a type that merely wraps *Store in an
+// unexported field (e.g. namespace storage, which has its own bespoke
+// IdentityByName filter) does not promote it and is correctly skipped. The
+// name is Deckhouse-specific to avoid any collision with upstream method
+// sets.
+func (e *Store) DeckhouseScopeStore() *Store {
+	return e
+}
